     "<div dir=\"ltr\">This is what I did if anyone is interested in this, please tell me if you think I am wrong <div><br></div><div><div>My question: <span style=\"white-space:pre\">\t</span># What percentage of promoters is covered by CpGs that are at least 10x covered ?</div><div><br></div><div>#get the number of gene features that have at least 1 CpG site that is well covered (&gt;10x)<br></div><div><span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,242,204)\">length(subsetByOverlaps(gene.obj$promoters,as(meth10x,&quot;GRanges&quot;)</span></div><div><br></div><div>#total number of promoters </div><div><span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,242,204)\">/length(gene.obj$promoters)<br></span></div><div><br></div><div>#percent promoters that are covered by the RRBS </div><div><span style=\"background-color:rgb(255,242,204)\">percentprom.coverage = length(subsetByOverlaps(gene.obj$promoters,as(meth10x,&quot;GRanges&quot;)))/length(gene.obj$promoters)</span></div><div><br></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><br>On Saturday, October 14, 2017 at 2:45:21 AM UTC-4, Altuna Akalin wrote:</div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">if you want to calculate what percentage of genomic features is covered by CpGs with methylation calls, you can just use methylRaw object, convert it to GRanges with as(...,&quot;GRanges&quot;). Then you can use GenomicRanges::findOverlaps or something similar to figure out what percentage of features are covered.<div><br></div><div>Best,</div><div>Altuna</div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Sat, Oct 14, 2017 at 1:37 AM, Linda Molla <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a rel=\"nofollow\">molla...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>I just want to clarify my question here. I am not interested in the coverage of just the CpG sites(which methyl kit provides). But other regions as well. What I need is to do something similar to the <b>figure1 A.B</b> of the paper below:</div><div><i><br></i></div><div><i>Base-Pair Resolution DNA Methylation Sequencing Reveals Profoundly Divergent Epigenetic Landscapes in Acute Myeloid Leukemia</i><br></div><div><i><a href=\"http://journals.plos.org/plosgenetics/article?id=10.1371/journal.pgen.1002781\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://journals.plos.org/plosgenetics/article?id%3D10.1371/journal.pgen.1002781&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793232242823000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2w6VN7wHM0D_6e_A-cEyLZ\">http://journals.plos.org/plosgenetics/article?id=10.1371/journal.pgen.1002781</a><br></i></div><div><i><br></i></div><div>I would really appreciate your help in this</div><div>Thank you</div><div>Linda</div><div><br></div><br></div></blockquote></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div><div>On Tue, Jul 11, 2017 at 8:29 AM, Altuna Akalin <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a rel=\"nofollow\">aak...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></div></div></div></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div><div><div dir=\"ltr\">you can use regionCounts() if you have the locations of the specific regions you are interested in. you can also coerce methylKit objects to GRanges objects and use findOverlaps or subsetByoverlaps from GenomicRanges package.</div></div></div></blockquote></div></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div><div><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><span>On Mon, Jul 10, 2017 at 8:54 PM, Linda Molla <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a rel=\"nofollow\">molla...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></span></div></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div><div><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><span><div dir=\"ltr\">I am interested to know if methylkit can be used to find out the coverage of specific regions in the genome. For example of all the potential enhancers or promoters rich in CpGs in a reference genome (example annotated promoters or enhancers in mouse genome)  how many of them are actually well covered through the ERRBS sequencing<div>If this is not possible through methylkit any recommendations on how to do this using the ERRBS data?</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>Thank you,</div><div>Linda</div><span><font color=\"#888888\"><div><br></div></font></span></div></span></blockquote></div></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
